# What `gcloud run deploy --source .` uploads to the Cloud Build staging
# bucket before the image is built.
#
# This is a security control, not only a size one. Without it the source
# tarball — including backend/.env and its live API keys — is written to a
# Cloud Storage bucket in the project, where it persists after the build and is
# readable by anyone with storage access. The Dockerfile's leak guard would
# then fail the build, correctly, but the secret would already have left the
# machine. So the exclusion has to happen here, one step earlier.
#
# SYNTAX IS .gitignore's, NOT .dockerignore's. gcloud applies gitignore
# semantics, so a bare pattern is re-applied at every directory level and
# `.env` does exclude `backend/.env`. .dockerignore in this repo is the
# opposite and is `**/`-anchored throughout for exactly that reason — see the
# header of that file, and REPAIR_REPORT.md §F for the build that shipped five
# live keys because the two were confused. Do not copy patterns between the
# two files.

# Secrets.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# Local virtualenvs. backend/.venv alone is ~417MB of host-specific binaries
# that would be uploaded on every deploy and then shadow the versions
# `pip install` resolves inside the image.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Caches, build output and local state.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
*.log
.DS_Store

# Runtime state, never built in. The response cache is created at the writable
# path resolved in backend/data/data_cache.py; fitted models are written by the
# retrain job.
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
backend/ml_models/*.joblib
backend/ml_models/*.pkl
backend/ml_models/*_trained_at.txt

# The frontend is deployed by Vercel, not by Cloud Run, and node_modules is
# the largest directory in the repository.
frontend/
node_modules/
.next/
out/

# Not needed to build or run the image.
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.vscode/
*.pdf

# Documentation is not part of the build. Excluding the directory is cheaper
# than relying on the *.pdf and *.md rules to cover everything in it, and it
# keeps the staging-bucket upload to the code that actually gets built.
docs/
backend/tests/
